    {"id": "mbale-cities-v2", "descriptionAdvanced": "Mbale, situated at the foot of Mount Elgon, is a central trade hub in Eastern Uganda known for its pleasant climate and coffee production. The city serves as an essential gateway to the Sipi Falls and other attractions in the Mount Elgon region. With a growing urban core, it supports the economic needs of the densely populated surrounding farming communities. Mbale remains a focal point for regional coffee trade, connecting local producers to broader markets. Geography K7 — Highland economies and agriculture.", "factsAdvanced": ["Located at the base of Mount Elgon.", "Known for high-quality Arabica coffee production.", "Regional gateway to the Sipi Falls.", "Key economic center in Eastern Uganda.", "Supports dense agricultural hinterlands.", "Known for a cooler mountain climate."]},
    {"id": "mukono-cities-v2", "descriptionAdvanced": "Mukono is a rapidly developing satellite city on the outskirts of Kampala, benefiting from its strategic location on the highway to Jinja. Once a small town, it has transitioned into a major urban center due to industrial and educational growth. It hosts several significant institutions and industrial sites that contribute to the region's employment and urban density. Its closeness to the capital has made it a preferred location for commuters and light industries. Geography K7 — Urban expansion and satellite cities.", "factsAdvanced": ["Strategically located between Kampala and Jinja.", "Rapid growth as a satellite city.", "Industrial hub with various manufacturing plants.", "Significant growth in educational facilities.", "Key transport node on the eastern highway.", "Increasingly residential in nature."]},
    {"id": "masaka-cities-v2", "descriptionAdvanced": "Masaka is a prominent city in Southern Uganda and a historical center of the Buganda region. It serves as a major transit point for trade between the southern districts and the capital, Kampala. The region is famous for its intensive banana and coffee cultivation, which form the core of the local economy. Masaka's urban layout and vibrant social atmosphere reflect its long-standing importance in the country's history and administration. Geography K7 — Regional trade hubs.", "factsAdvanced": ["Historical significance in the Buganda region.", "Major transit center in Southern Uganda.", "Core economy based on banana and coffee farming.", "Strategically located on the main southern trade route.", "Dynamic urban social and economic life.", "Key administrative hub in Masaka District."]},
    {"id": "kasese-cities-v2", "descriptionAdvanced": "Kasese, nestled in the foothills of the Rwenzori Mountains in Western Uganda, is an essential center for mining and tourism. The region is known for its mineral wealth, particularly copper and cobalt, which has shaped its industrial landscape. Its proximity to the Queen Elizabeth National Park makes it a primary base for tourists visiting the country's most famous wildlife areas. The city serves as a unique economic bridge between the high mountains and the surrounding plains. Geography K7 — Mining and nature-based tourism.", "factsAdvanced": ["Known for its historical copper and cobalt mining.", "Proximity to the Rwenzori Mountains and Queen Elizabeth National Park.", "Primary tourist gateway in Western Uganda.", "Economic center defined by industrial and wildlife tourism.", "Unique geographical location between plains and mountains.", "Growing base for adventurous travel."]},
    {"id": "hoima-cities-v2", "descriptionAdvanced": "Hoima, in Western Uganda, has gained significant national prominence due to the recent discovery and exploration of oil in the Albertine Graben. This has spurred rapid infrastructure growth and economic development in the region, attracting substantial investment. Historically, it was the seat of the Bunyoro Kingdom, maintaining a deep cultural connection to its heritage. Hoima is now a focal point for both energy sector expansion and traditional agricultural activities. Geography K7 — Energy resources and national development.", "factsAdvanced": ["Central hub for oil exploration in the Albertine Graben.", "Historically the capital of the Bunyoro Kingdom.", "Rapidly developing urban infrastructure due to the oil sector.", "Economic transition from agriculture to energy-driven growth.", "Gateway to Murchison Falls National Park.", "Significant cultural and historical site in Uganda."]},
    {"id": "arua-cities-v2", "descriptionAdvanced": "Arua, in the far northwest of Uganda, serves as the commercial center of the West Nile sub-region. Its proximity to the borders of the Democratic Republic of the Congo and South Sudan makes it a bustling hub for international trade. The city has experienced significant growth as a trading post, facilitating both legal cross-border commerce and regional distribution. Arua reflects a diverse social environment influenced by its unique location and history. Geography K7 — Cross-border trade hubs.", "factsAdvanced": ["Commercial hub of the West Nile sub-region.", "Major transit point for trade with DRC and South Sudan.", "Economic hub characterized by diverse cross-border commerce.", "Strategically important for regional food and supply distribution.", "Cultural melting pot of Northern and border communities.", "Rapid expansion as an urban trade center."]},
    {"id": "soroti-cities-v2", "descriptionAdvanced": "Soroti, in Eastern Uganda, is the primary economic center for the Teso sub-region. It is famous for its citrus fruit production and as a major transit point for travelers moving across the northern and eastern parts of the country. The city's landscape is defined by its characteristic rock outcrops and diverse agricultural activities. Soroti has evolved into a key service hub, providing essential support to the surrounding rural and agricultural districts. Geography K7 — Agriculture-based urban centers.", "factsAdvanced": ["Main urban hub of the Teso sub-region.", "Well-known for extensive citrus fruit production.", "Key transit point for northern and eastern travel.", "Landscaped by unique local rock formations.", "Primary service center for regional rural districts.", "Diverse agricultural base featuring crops and livestock."]},
    {"id": "fort-portal-cities-v2", "descriptionAdvanced": "Fort Portal, in Western Uganda, is known for its stunning green scenery, crater lakes, and proximity to the Rwenzori Mountains. Often described as one of the most beautiful towns in Uganda, it is a key destination for eco-tourism and adventure travel. The city maintains a cooler climate and a laid-back atmosphere, contrasting with the intense urban activity of Kampala. It also serves as the headquarters of the Tooro Kingdom, rich in cultural heritage. Geography K7 — Sustainable tourism and regional landscapes.", "factsAdvanced": ["Scenic town located in Western Uganda.", "Famous for its numerous surrounding crater lakes.", "Headquarters of the cultural Tooro Kingdom.", "Popular destination for mountain tourism and eco-travel.", "Pleasant cooler climate throughout the year.", "Key gateway to the Kibale Forest National Park."]},
    {"id": "tororo-cities-v2", "descriptionAdvanced": "Tororo, in Eastern Uganda, is historically recognized as an industrial town, famous for its iconic rock and significant limestone mining operations. It serves as a major junction for the railway and road trade between Uganda and Kenya. The town's economy is deeply linked to cement production and other industrial activities. Its prominent geographical feature, Tororo Rock, is a symbol of the region and a local landmark. Geography K7 — Industrial urban landscapes.", "factsAdvanced": ["Home to the iconic and visually striking Tororo Rock.", "Important industrial center for cement and limestone production.", "Major road and rail hub for trade with Kenya.", "Historically significant for early industrial development.", "Strategic junction for cross-border logistics.", "Regional symbol of industrial and natural integration."]},
    {"id": "lugazi-cities-v2", "descriptionAdvanced": "Lugazi, on the Kampala-Jinja highway, is synonymous with large-scale sugar production, dominated by the presence of the Lugazi Sugar Works. The city's economy and social structure revolve around the extensive sugar cane plantations that cover much of the surrounding land. This has fostered a unique industrial town character with deep roots in the commercial agricultural sector. Lugazi remains a critical node in the nation's sugar supply chain and regional transport. Geography K7 — Commercial agriculture and industrial towns.", "factsAdvanced": ["Industrial center known for massive sugar cane plantations.", "Home to the historic Lugazi Sugar Works.", "Economic life centered around sugar cane production.", "Vital node on the main highway between Kampala and Jinja.", "Unique industrial character and worker communities.", "Essential producer in Uganda's sugar industry."]},
    {"id": "mityana-cities-v2", "descriptionAdvanced": "Mityana, located along the road to Western Uganda, is a rapidly growing urban center serving as a key market and service hub for the surrounding districts. It is known for its role in agricultural trading, particularly coffee and food crop distribution. Mityana's growth is driven by its function as a central stop for travelers and local merchants in the Central region. The town balances its agricultural roots with increasing commercial activity. Geography K7 — Market town development.", "factsAdvanced": ["Strategic transit town in Central Uganda.", "Market hub for regional coffee and staple food trading.", "Central service point for rural agricultural districts.", "Rapid urban growth driven by commercial transit.", "Active social and economic life for the local region.", "Important link in the regional supply chain."]},
    {"id": "iganga-cities-v2", "descriptionAdvanced": "Iganga, in Eastern Uganda, is a busy commercial town and a crucial stop on the main highway toward the Kenyan border. It thrives as a vibrant market center, where agricultural produce from the fertile surrounding lands is bought, sold, and redistributed. The town's strategic importance is highlighted by the high volume of transit goods moving through it daily. It serves as a primary hub for economic interactions in the Busoga sub-region. Geography K7 — Market town trade routes.", "factsAdvanced": ["Busy commercial center on the way to the Kenyan border.", "Key hub for agricultural produce trading.", "High volume of transit trade and logistics.", "Central market town in the Busoga sub-region.", "Hub for regional rural economic interaction.", "Strategically located for cross-country movement."]},
    {"id": "busia-cities-v2", "descriptionAdvanced": "Busia, on the border with Kenya, is a high-stakes trade city primarily defined by its role as a key international border crossing point. It is a hive of activity where goods and people flow between East African countries daily. The economy of Busia is built on logistics, cross-border services, and diverse small-scale trading. Its proximity to the border brings a distinct international character to its urban life and social fabric. Geography K7 — International border trade hubs.", "factsAdvanced": ["Crucial international border crossing point with Kenya.", "Economic engine based on logistics and cross-border trade.", "Dynamic hub for regional trade and transit services.", "High level of interaction between Ugandan and Kenyan business communities.", "Diverse small-scale trading and service economy.", "Vital checkpoint for international movement and trade."]},
    {"id": "busembatia-cities-v2", "descriptionAdvanced": "Busembatia is a central commercial town in Eastern Uganda, located in the heart of the Busoga region, known for its vibrant markets. The city provides essential services and market space for the dense surrounding rural population, enabling significant economic exchange. It acts as a vital link in the local agricultural supply chain, particularly for crops harvested in the fertile plains of the east. Geography K6 — Rural market hubs.", "factsAdvanced": ["Key commercial center in the Busoga region.", "Active regional agricultural markets.", "Strategically located in the Iganga district.", "Economy primarily supported by maize and rice farming.", "Serves as a local hub for produce distribution.", "Known for its traditional and welcoming community."]},
    {"id": "kitgum-cities-v2", "descriptionAdvanced": "Kitgum, in Northern Uganda, is a resilient urban center that has played an important role in the post-conflict reconstruction of the region. As a major town in the north, it provides services, health care, and trade infrastructure to a vast area. Its development is a testament to the growth and renewal of Northern Uganda. The town remains a focal point for both local agriculture and regional economic interaction. Geography K7 — Post-conflict urban renewal.", "factsAdvanced": ["Key urban center in Northern Uganda.", "Important role in regional post-conflict reconstruction.", "Provider of essential services and healthcare for the north.", "Focal point for agricultural and regional trade.", "Symbol of growth and community resilience.", "Vital hub for regional northern connectivity."]},
    {"id": "nansana-cities-v2", "descriptionAdvanced": "Nansana, a densely populated suburb and town on the outskirts of Kampala, is one of the fastest-growing urban areas in the metropolitan region. Its growth is primarily driven by the expansion of the capital, serving as a residential and commercial base for a massive commuter population. The town reflects the rapid urbanization currently unfolding in Central Uganda. It is an area characterized by diverse businesses, small industries, and increasing infrastructure development. Geography K7 — Urban sprawl and metropolitan growth.", "factsAdvanced": ["One of the fastest-growing residential and commercial suburbs of Kampala.", "Densely populated and rapidly urbanizing region.", "Primary base for a large commuter population working in the capital.", "Diverse hub of small businesses and light industries.", "Continuous expansion fueled by urban sprawl from Kampala.", "Significant investment in local infrastructure."] },
    {"id": "kira-cities-v2", "descriptionAdvanced": "Kira, another massive urban expansion area in the Kampala Metropolitan region, is essentially a city defined by residential growth and commercial vitality. Its rapid transition from semi-rural land to a major urban area showcases the intense demand for housing and services. It serves as a modern base for a large number of professionals who commute to Kampala. Kira represents the changing face of Ugandan urban living, blending modern development with regional demographic trends. Geography K7 — Urban planning and metropolitan sprawl.", "factsAdvanced": ["Major urban residential and commercial municipality.", "Key part of the rapidly growing Kampala Metropolitan region.", "Primarily residential hub for professionals.", "Reflects the intense demand for housing in Central Uganda.", "Modern urban planning and development projects.", "Continuous transition from rural land to vibrant city."] },
